"Defines the required ordering of participant interactions without exposing each party's internal workflow.\n\nUse when: Agree a shared business exchange protocol. Review permissible message sequences before partners design their procedures.\n\nAvoid when: Internal responsibilities, resource assignments or local processing details are central.\n\nVisual grammar: Choreography tasks represent interactions with participant bands. The initiating participant band is unshaded; responding bands are shaded. Events and gateways order interaction tasks rather than internal work.\n\nBuild: Choose the participants and the public exchange boundary. Name each interaction and identify its initiating participant. Add alternatives and concurrency using choreography sequence semantics.\n\nCheck: Each interaction has an initiator and receiving participant. The sequence describes shared observable interactions only. Gateway choices are supported by information participants can know.\n\nPitfalls: Confusing participant bands with ordinary swimlanes. Putting private work such as inventory counting into a choreography task.\n\nExample: A purchase protocol requires quote request, quote response and either order acceptance or withdrawal, regardless of partners' internal procedures.\n\nAlternatives: BPMN collaboration, BPMN conversation."
